; `set` on a STRUCT FIELD the declaration did not mark `mut`. Until
; 2026-08-31 this passed `check` in silence and the program ran: the
; parser recognised `mut` on a field, skipped it, and recorded nothing
; - `parseOneField` said so in as many words - so there was nothing
; for the checker to read and every field of every struct was
; writable. AX2004's own explain text states what that costs: "a
; marker that reads as an ownership guarantee and supplies none is
; worse than no marker, because a reader spends trust on it."
;
; Same code as the immutable-BINDING refusal, and the same rule: a
; field is immutable unless it says otherwise. docs/memory-model.md
; MM-MUT-2a.
;
; THREE WRITES, ONE DIAGNOSTIC - the two silent ones are what keep
; this from being a check that refuses everything:
;
;   1  `(set p.ok 1)` on a `mut` field           silent
;   2  `(set o.inn.v 2)` - `inn` is NOT `mut`    silent, because only
;      the LAST segment is governed: the store mutates the `Inner` at
;      `inn`, not the `inn` slot
;   3  `(set p.no 3)` on a plain field           AX3012, anchored on
;      `no` in the write and not on the declaration, which may be in
;      another unit
(struct Inner
  (mut v : Int))

(struct Outer
  (inn : Inner))

(struct P
  (mut ok : Int)
  (no : Int))

(:: main Int)

(fn (main)
  (let (
    (p (P 0 0))
    (o (Outer (Inner 0)))
  )
    {
      (set p.ok 1)
      (set o.inn.v 2)
      (set p.no 3)
      (+ p.ok o.inn.v)
    }
  )
)
